What I checked
• git show --stat HEAD → the commit under evaluation touches **one file**: dreams/experiments.jsonl, 1 insertion. No Rust source, and git status --porcelain is clean.
• `cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings` → exit 0, no warnings. So there is no lint failure at all; the reported failure was a test assertion, not a lint.
• `cargo test commands_file::tests::test_suggest_related_test_file_detection` → passes.
• Full cargo test re-run → 4939 passed; 0 failed; 1 ignored, plus the three green integration/gate binaries.

Why it flaked
src/commands_file.rs:2667-2676 mutates process-wide state: it calls std::env::set_current_dir(dir.path()), runs suggest_related_files, then restores. It's marked #[serial], but serial only orders it against other #[serial] tests — any concurrently running non-serial test that reads or changes the cwd can knock it over. Its sibling test_suggest_related_module_parent (same pattern, 20 lines below) already flaked this way on 2026-08-12 per the project memory log.

So: pre-existing cwd-race flake in a test the diff never touched. Making a "targeted fix" to src/commands_file.rs would mean editing a source file for a task whose entire specification is zero edits under `src/` — that is the exact bundling failure that ate the previous four attempts at this grade (git reset --hard takes the ledger line with it).

Verdict is unchanged: PASS. session_plan/eval_task_1.md stands as written. If the flaky test is worth hardening (e.g. a cwd-injecting parameter instead of set_current_dir), that's a separate task and should be filed as its own issue.
